---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioSplitGif from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioSplitGif.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="split-gif">
  <GifAudioSplitGif client:load />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to split a GIF into frames</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop the GIF in. Its dimensions, frame count and total length appear at once, read from the file header without decoding.</li>
      <li>Choose PNG or JPEG, and whether you want every frame or a sample.</li>
      <li>Press extract. Each frame appears in the contact sheet with its own delay and running timestamp.</li>
      <li>Save one frame with the button under its thumbnail, or download the whole set as a ZIP.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Where the frame delays come from</h2>
    <p>
      A GIF stores timing per frame, not as a frame rate: before each image there is a Graphic Control
      Extension block with a delay in hundredths of a second. This page reads those blocks directly from the
      file bytes, so the timings shown are exactly what the file specifies. A tool that only asks the decoder
      gets an average from a nominal frame rate.
    </p>
    <p>
      This matters often. Hand-made GIFs often hold a title frame for two seconds and then run the rest at
      40 ms, and screen recordings from capture tools often have irregular timing where nothing moved. A
      uniform "10 fps" readout hides this; the per-frame table shows it.
    </p>

    <h2>What is in the ZIP</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Each extracted frame, numbered in playback order with zero-padded names so they sort correctly in any file manager.</li>
      <li><code>frame-delays.csv</code>, with one row per frame: its number, its frame number in the source, its delay in milliseconds, its running timestamp and its byte size.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      With the CSV you can rebuild the animation. Edit or delete some frames, then rebuild with the
      <a href="/images-to-gif">GIF maker</a> and enter the delays from the CSV. The result plays at exactly
      the original speed.
    </p>

    <h2>PNG or JPEG?</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th></th><th>PNG</th><th>JPEG</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Quality</td><td>Lossless: a pixel-exact copy of the decoded frame</td><td>Lossy, but at quality 2 the loss is very hard to see</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Transparency</td><td>Kept as a real alpha channel</td><td>Not supported: transparent pixels become black</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Size</td><td>Small for flat colour, large for photographic content</td><td>Smaller for photographic content</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Use it for</td><td>Almost everything: screenshots, line art, logos, anything you will edit</td><td>Hundreds of photographic frames you only need to look through</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      GIF transparency is binary: a pixel is fully opaque or fully invisible. So a PNG exported here has a
      hard-edged alpha channel. This comes from the source format, not from the extraction.
    </p>

    <h2>Sampling long animations</h2>
    <p>
      Extraction stops at 600 frames so the browser can hold all decoded images and stay responsive. For
      longer animations, use the "every 2nd frame" or "every 5th frame" option. Sampling happens during
      decoding, so it covers the whole animation and does not stop at frame 600. For a long screen recording
      where consecutive frames barely differ, a sampled contact sheet is often more useful than the full set.
    </p>

    <h2>Uses for split GIF frames</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Finding the best still: extract a reaction GIF and pick the one frame that works as a static image.</li>
      <li>Fixing an animation: extract the frames, delete or retouch the bad ones, and rebuild.</li>
      <li>Sprite sheets: numbered PNGs are what a game engine’s animation importer expects.</li>
      <li>Frame-by-frame study: step through a captured bug or a demonstration one frame at a time, with the real timestamps.</li>
      <li>Rebuilding at a different speed: extract, then reassemble with a uniform delay to even out irregular timing.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Runs on your device</h2>
    <p>
      FFmpeg, compiled to WebAssembly, decodes the GIF in this tab, and JSZip builds the ZIP here too. There
      is no file size limit other than your device’s memory, and no watermark. After the engine is cached
      once, the page works with the network off.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
